&lt;p&gt;Join the &lt;a href="http://www.publichealth.columbia.edu/research/centers/robert-n-butler-columbia-aging-center" target="_blank"&gt;Robert N. Butler Columbia Aging Center&lt;/a&gt; for&amp;nbsp;the next in a series focusing&amp;nbsp;on the applications of AI in aging.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Hear from &lt;a href="https://www.discovery.co.za/corporate/adrian-gore-biography" target="_blank"&gt;Adrian Gore&lt;/a&gt;, Founder and Group Chief Executive of Discovery, the South Africa&amp;ndash;based insurer behind the global Vitality model. Gore shares Discovery&amp;rsquo;s core insight about its shared-value insurance approach: &amp;ldquo;When people get healthier, they benefit &amp;ndash; and so do we. When our members thrive, we&amp;rsquo;re more profitable.&amp;rdquo; He will introduce Vitality AI, Discovery&amp;rsquo;s new platform built with Google, designed to use advanced AI and rich health behavior data to help people live longer, healthier lives. Discovery is now partnering with Columbia Mailman School of Public Health to extend this impact even further.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Welcome &amp;amp; Remarks will be given by Columbia Aging Center faculty:&amp;nbsp;&lt;br /&gt;
Interim Director &lt;a href="https://www.publichealth.columbia.edu/profile/allison-aiello-phd" target="_blank"&gt;Allison Aiello, PhD&lt;/a&gt;, and event Co-Hosts&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://www.publichealth.columbia.edu/profile/alan-cohen-phd" target="_blank"&gt;Alan Cohen, PhD&lt;/a&gt;, and &lt;a href="https://www.publichealth.columbia.edu/profile/ying-wei-phd" target="_blank"&gt;Ying Wei, PhD&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://columbiacuimc.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_pl7PfohwQFKBCH0OBTNbDA#/registration" target="_blank"&gt;REGISTER HERE&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr /&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;THE ROBERT N. BUTLER COLUMBIA AGING CENTER VIRTUAL SERIES | AI + HEALTHY LONGEVITY&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Our current series is dedicated to &amp;quot;&lt;a href="https://www.publichealth.columbia.edu/research/centers/robert-n-butler-columbia-aging-center/news-events/current-series-ai-healthy-longevity" target="_blank"&gt;AI + Healthy Longevity&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;quot; The rise of artificial intelligence is set to transform health research, offering unprecedented opportunities for innovation, accuracy, and efficiency. As AI becomes more deeply integrated into research and healthcare, its applications hold the power to unlock new ways of improving health outcomes across the life course &amp;ndash; particularly in later life. From predicting risks to preventing disease and preserving function, the use of AI to promote healthy longevity is only just beginning.&amp;nbsp; Yet alongside these opportunities come important challenges: ensuring data quality, promoting transparency, and addressing ethical concerns.&amp;nbsp; The Columbia Aging Center&amp;rsquo;s 2025&amp;ndash;2026 virtual seminar series, AI + Healthy Longevity, will spotlight these critical issues. Through cutting-edge talks by leading experts &amp;ndash; from population health to medical sciences &amp;ndash;this series will explore how AI is shaping the future of aging research and advancing the quest for healthier, longer lives.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;These events are open to Columbia faculty, staff, students, alumni, and the public.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;Rights, Evidence, and Reciprocal Learning: Structural Interventions for Adolescent Health, Globally and at Home&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;em&gt;Audrey Pettifor, PhD&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Gillings School of Global Public Health&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Despite broad agreement that structural factors shape adolescent vulnerability to HIV, rigorous evidence on whether rights‑based interventions truly improve outcomes remains limited. Drawing on more than two decades of epidemiological research across Southern Africa and the U.S.&amp;mdash;including landmark studies on cash transfers, community mobilization, and long‑acting PrEP access for LGBTQ+ youth&amp;mdash;Dr. Pettifor will examine what the evidence actually shows and how structural, rights‑based approaches can strengthen adolescent health. A central theme is reciprocal learning: what the U.S. can take from high‑burden settings, and why rights‑based frameworks and rigorous epidemiology must work hand in hand. &lt;strong&gt;Join us to see why structural interventions may be the most powerful&amp;mdash;and overlooked&amp;mdash;tool we have.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;About the Presenter&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Audrey Pettifor, PhD, MPH, is a Professor and Associate Chair whose work focuses on HIV prevention and care among adolescents and young adults globally. A social epidemiologist with long-standing experience in South Africa, she has led extensive NIH-funded research, published over 300 peer‑reviewed articles, and serves as an Honorary Professor at the University of the Witwatersrand. She co-leads a Fogarty-funded implementation science training program, is a member of AHISA, and holds leadership roles within the UNC CFAR. Her current work includes a U.S. Adolescent Trials Network study evaluating home and community-based PrEP distribution. She brings deep expertise in HIV prevention and implementation science across Africa and the U.S.&lt;/p&gt;
